facts:
Replacing $x$ by $-x$ gives $y = f(-x)$, a reflection in the $y$-axis, so each point $(x, y)$ moves to $(-x, y)$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.2]
\draw[->] (-2.6,0) -- (2.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.0) -- (0,2.0) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, gray, <->, domain=-2.2:1.6, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.4*\x*\x*\x + 0.5*\x*\x - 1.2*\x - 0.6});
\draw[thick, blue, <->, domain=-1.6:2.2, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {-0.4*\x*\x*\x + 0.5*\x*\x + 1.2*\x - 0.6});
\node[gray] at (-1.5,1.55) {$y=f(x)$};
\node[blue] at (1.5,-1.55) {$y=f(-x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Replacing $y$ by $-y$ instead gives $y = -f(x)$, a reflection in the $x$-axis, so each point $(x, y)$ moves to $(x, -y)$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.2]
\draw[->] (-2.6,0) -- (2.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.0) -- (0,2.0) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, gray, <->, domain=-2.2:1.6, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.4*\x*\x*\x + 0.5*\x*\x - 1.2*\x - 0.6});
\draw[thick, blue, <->, domain=-2.2:1.6, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {-0.4*\x*\x*\x - 0.5*\x*\x + 1.2*\x + 0.6});
\node[gray] at (-1.5,1.55) {$y=f(x)$};
\node[blue] at (1.5,-1.55) {$y=-f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Replacing both gives $y = -f(-x)$, sending each point $(x, y)$ to $(-x, -y)$, which is the same as a $180^\circ$ rotation about the origin.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.2]
\draw[->] (-2.8,0) -- (2.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.0) -- (0,2.0) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, gray, <->, domain=-2.2:1.6, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.4*\x*\x*\x + 0.5*\x*\x - 1.2*\x - 0.6});
\draw[thick, blue, <->, domain=-1.6:2.2, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.4*\x*\x*\x - 0.5*\x*\x - 1.2*\x + 0.6});
\node[gray] at (-1.6,1.55) {$y=f(x)$};
\node[blue] at (1.6,-1.55) {$y=-f(-x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
The parabola $y = (x - 3)^2 - 2$ has its vertex at $(3, -2)$.
Find the vertex of the parabola after reflection across the $x$-axis.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.65]
\draw[->] (-1.5,0) -- (6.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.2) -- (0,6.8) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-1,1,2,3,4,5,6} {
  \draw (\x,0.15) -- (\x,-0.15) node[below, font=\scriptsize] {$\x$};
}
\foreach \y in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5,6} {
  \draw (0.15,\y) -- (-0.15,\y) node[left, font=\scriptsize] {$\y$};
}
\node[below left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,0) {$0$};
\fill (3,-2) circle (2.5pt);
\draw[thick, <->, domain=0.25:5.75, samples=60, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {(\x-3)*(\x-3)-2});
\node[right] at (5.2, 3.8) {$y=f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

mastery.question_text:
The graph of $y = f(x)$ has $x$-intercepts at $x = -2$ and $x = 5$, and a $y$-intercept at $y = -10$.
Determine all intercepts of the transformed graph $y = -f(-x)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.45]
\draw[->] (-3.8,0) -- (6.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-13.8) -- (0,6.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5,6} {
  \draw (\x,0.2) -- (\x,-0.2) node[below, font=\scriptsize] {$\x$};
}
\foreach \y in {-12,-10,-8,-6,-4,-2,2,4,6} {
  \draw (0.2,\y) -- (-0.2,\y) node[left, font=\scriptsize] {$\y$};
}
\node[below left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-2.6:5.6, samples=60, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {(\x+2)*(\x-5)});
\fill (-2,0) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (5,0) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (0,-10) circle (2.5pt);
\node[right] at (3.5,-5) {$y=f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
